  "conclusions": [
    "Both services/input remain READY with PRESENT grants, unchanged service starts and the original authenticated connection for about 388 seconds. Zero new heartbeat failure, reconnect, fault or recovery-required event in retained postconnection logs; older logs are incomplete.",
    "B first entry is 25.018ms with an already prepared candidate aged 316.050s, versus the prior first-entry 267.456ms with an unfinished builder. This supports that connection-triggered preparation completed before this entry. All eleven new ready waits lie in 22.267..26.853ms; median 24.2645 -> 24.054ms, maximum 267.456 -> 26.853ms.",
    "A capture maximum decreases 123.413 -> 86.205ms, but median increases 20.504 -> 33.709ms. A first foreground request median increases 11.5805 -> 16.824ms, maximum 35.305 -> 31.956ms. This trial does not establish an overall activation speedup or a causal benefit from the priority hint.",
    "The slowest A capture now has material agent admission, worker validation and command-dispatch intervals before its 23.650ms activation. The remaining delay cannot be attributed to foreground activation alone. Tiny matching local pipe request/reply delivery does not explain the entire outlier.",
    "B worst-input maximum increases 7.138 -> 22.361ms; exactly one of 1614 receiver dispatches is in the 10-to-under-100ms bucket, none is at least 100ms. Native application in that matching event is 4.032ms, while service request-to-read is 13.503ms. This is a remaining intermittent input issue despite the user reporting no problem.",
    "TCP RTT median is similar (5.1825 -> 5.1745ms), but the maximum estimate rises 8.178 -> 67.456ms. All eleven matched leases have known zero retransmitted-byte/timeout-episode deltas. RTT is an estimate, not the measured network contribution to one lease grant.",
    "Eleven complete current profiles versus twelve prior profiles under uncontrolled load are a small comparison. First-offer maximum falls 326.547 -> 130.863ms while median rises 61.5455 -> 86.443ms; do not claim uniformly seamless transfer or statistical proof."
  ],
